+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdMPD9dKNyDmUmMqsSmnw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012fVkBwcJiNq1i1ho3hpKpT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/README.md","content":"# Factory Reset\n\nSolvers for the two button-toggle puzzles described in `TASK.md`.\n\n```ts\nimport { solveToggle, solveCounter } from './src/index.js';\n\nsolveToggle({ target: [true, false], buttons: [[0], [0, 1]] }); // [0]\nsolveCounter([2, 2], [[0, 1], [0], [1]], 5); // [2, 0, 0]\n```\n\nBoth puzzles are linear systems, so both solvers start with Gaussian\nelimination — over GF(2) for the toggles, over Z_p for the counters. That\nsettles solvability immediately and reduces the machine to a particular\nsolution plus a null space; the interesting part is picking the *cheapest*\nmember of that solution set, which is a minimum-weight coset problem (NP-hard\nin general). Each solver therefore carries two searches and picks by shape.\n\n## Part 1 — `solveToggle` (`src/toggle.ts`)\n\nButtons and lights are bitmasks in a pair of 30-bit words, so a machine at the\nstated limits is a 40x40 system over GF(2).\n\n| null space | search | cost |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `k <= 24` | Gray-code walk of all `2^k` solutions, keeping the lightest | `2^k` cheap steps |\n| `k > 24` | meet in the middle over the two halves of the buttons | `2^(M/2)` |\n\nThe second path only runs when the null space is huge, which means the rank is\nsmall (`rank = M - k < 16`), so the halves can be tabulated in a direct-address\narray indexed by light pattern rather than a hash map.\n\nEvery 40-button machine finishes in well under 100ms; the worst case is a null\nspace of exactly 24 (~60ms).\n\n## Part 2 — `solveCounter` (`src/counter.ts`)\n\nIdentical buttons are collapsed first: presses can always be moved onto the\nlast member of such a group without raising the total, which both shrinks the\nproblem and settles the lexicographic tie-break for those buttons.\n\n| shape | search | cost |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `p^k <= 10^6` | depth-first over the free variables | ~`p^k`, usually far less |\n| otherwise, `p^rank <= 10^6` | dynamic program over counter states | `M * p^rank` |\n\nThe coset search assigns free variables from the last to the first. A pivot's\npress count is forced as soon as the free variables it references are all\nfixed, so the running total is a valid lower bound and prunes the tree. Two\nthings make that bound bite: the elimination deliberately spreads the free\ncolumns evenly across the buttons (so pivots start closing early instead of\nall at the end), and a greedy descent seeds the incumbent before the search\nstarts. Spreading the free columns is worth ~100x on a 40x40 machine mod 97\nwith a null space of 4 (3.6s to 30ms).\n\nThe state search is the mirror image, for machines with far more buttons than\nindependent counters. Walking the buttons backwards, `cost[s]` is the cheapest\nway to cover state `s` with the remaining buttons; pressing a button `v` times\nwalks `v` steps around the cycle `s, s - col, s - 2*col, ...`, and a two-pass\nsweep of each cycle prices every `v` at once, so a button costs `O(p^rank)`\nrather than `O(p^(rank+1))`.\n\n### Known limits\n\nA machine whose rank *and* null space are both large — say 20 counters, 40\nbuttons, mod 97 — is out of reach: `p^k` and `p^rank` are both astronomical,\nand minimum-weight coset decoding has no polynomial algorithm. In that regime\nthe optimum also sits near the mean cost of a random solution, so there is no\nshort certificate to find. Everything within the stated limits that is not in\nthat regime (full rank, small null space, or small rank) is exact and fast;\n`solveCounter` stays under a second for the shapes listed in the tables above.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Buttons naming a light/counter index past the end of `target`/`lights` are\n  honoured: the extra positions exist and must end at zero.\n- Repeated indices inside one button count, so `[0, 0]` toggles light 0 twice\n  (a no-op) and increments counter 0 by two.\n- Both entry points take an optional trailing strategy argument\n  (`'auto'` by default). It exists so the tests can run every search path\n  against brute force on inputs small enough to enumerate.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm test        # unit tests, brute-force cross-checks, timing guards\nnpm run build   # tsc\nnpm run lint    # eslint\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":131,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":154073,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":278},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7a1c09e-f65c-48b9-a364-aeec4a117450","uuid":"a6925731-8d80-445f-bec5-5c1975fcb1d2","request_id":"req_011CdMPD7ybUgS8FbtZP6chA"}
